before:
new TimedTask(title,minutes) receives a title string and finite nonnegative minutes and constructs an instance. describe() takes no explicit arguments and returns text without changing the task. instanceof tests the instance’s prototype relationship; it does not copy or convert it.
```javascript
class Task {
  constructor(title) { this.title = title; }
  describe() { return "Task: " + this.title; }
}
class TimedTask extends Task {
  constructor(title, minutes) {
    super(title);
    this.minutes = minutes;
  }
  describe() {
    return super.describe() + " / " + this.minutes + " min";
  }
}
const task = new TimedTask("Read", 15);
console.log(task.describe());
console.log(task instanceof Task);
```
Output: Task: Read / 15 min, then true.

after:
Here is the complete example.
```javascript
class Task {
  constructor(title) { this.title = title; }
  describe() { return "Task: " + this.title; }
}
class TimedTask extends Task {
  constructor(title, minutes) {
    super(title);
    this.minutes = minutes;
  }
  describe() {
    return super.describe() + " / " + this.minutes + " min";
  }
}
const task = new TimedTask("Read", 15);
console.log(task.describe());
console.log(task instanceof Task);
```
Output:
```text
Task: Read / 15 min
true
```
`super(title)` saves the title through `Task`. The next line saves the minutes. `instanceof Task` checks the link to the parent class.
